        'en' => [
            'title' => 'A threat to life does not replace consent. Emergency care under the statute requires two conditions.',
            'image_alt' => 'A purple room. On the table, a sheet with one dark stroke and a hand lifting away from it. To the right, a silhouette in a hospital gown with a teal tube along the shoulder and a fainter second outline. Above them, one solid teal arc and one broken.',
            'excerpt' => 'The fourth volume of Sunday Metaphysics is ready in the library for Sunday 25 October 2026. This essay is not a shortened copy of it. Prior consent, today’s refusal, and the emergency exception come apart in it.',
            'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>For Sunday 25 October 2026 the fourth volume of <a href="library.php?slug=metafyzika-4"><em>Nedeľná metafyzika</em></a> (Sunday Metaphysics) is ready in the library. The text and the sources were checked on 24 October. Thirty pages follow the limits of help, withdrawal of consent, the family, and care at the end of a life. Here is the distinction that disappears first at the bedside.</p>
<p>Two days ago the record contained a consent. Today the patient denies the conversation, withdraws consent, and raises his voice. Three conclusions form at once: he is lying, he cannot decide, and because life is at stake the old signature is enough. Each conclusion is a separate question. A dispute about the past must not cover what he wants now, and whether he can decide about it.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.slov-lex.sk/pravne-predpisy/SK/ZZ/2004/576/">Act No. 576/2004 Coll.</a>, in the wording in force from 1 August to 30 December 2026, places two duties side by side. Section 4(3) requires the provider to give health care correctly: to perform the procedures needed to identify the disease and to give timely and effective treatment, according to current knowledge and standard procedures, taking account of the person’s individual state. Section 4(4) requires informed consent unless the Act provides otherwise. A clinical judgment that a procedure would help does not replace consent. Section 6(8) gives everyone who may give consent the right to withdraw it freely at any time. A signature documents a moment of deciding. It does not transfer the body.</p>
<p>The exception for emergency care is in section 6(9)(a). Consent is not required if it cannot be obtained in time, but it can be presumed. Both conditions must be met. An informed refusal is not a case in which consent cannot be obtained. Section 6(2) requires information that is intelligible, considerate, and free of pressure, with the opportunity and enough time to decide. Repeating the same sentences until the person is exhausted does not meet that requirement.</p>
<p>If the patient later cannot express a wish, <a href="https://www.slov-lex.sk/pravne-predpisy/SK/ZZ/2000/40/">Article 9 of the Convention on Human Rights and Biomedicine</a>, published by Notification No. 40/2000 Coll., says that account must be taken of a wish he expressed earlier about the intervention. That sentence does not install a detailed Slovak system of binding forms for every future circumstance. Loss of consciousness does not erase an earlier refusal. What he refused, on what information, and whether it applies to today’s situation still have to be judged.</p>
<p>Refusal of effective dialysis, impaired decision-making, and disproportionate treatment look alike from the outside. Dialysis is not started, and the person may die. The reason is different each time. Effective treatment that has been validly refused does not become futile by that refusal. Futility has to be tied to a goal the procedure cannot reach. Lowering the potassium concentration is one goal. Restoring consciousness after irreversible brain injury is another. Incapacity to decide cannot be inferred from disagreement. Fluent speech and a clear “no” do not rule out delirium. A diagnosis of depression, kidney failure, or dementia does not by itself prove incapacity to decide.</p>
<p>The British guideline <a href="https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg103">NICE CG103</a>, updated on 18 January 2023, recommends assessment with the 4AT when features of delirium are present. In critical care, and in the recovery room after surgery, it recommends CAM-ICU or the ICDSC instead. The result of a tool does not replace a clinical diagnosis, or a judgment of whether the person can decide about this procedure at this time. NICE remains a British guideline. A Slovak authorization does not follow from it. Capacity to decide is judged for a particular decision at a particular time. A standard that treats only agreement with the physician as reasonable grants autonomy only to those who do not use it.</p>
<p>The shared label “passive euthanasia” glues these reasons together. The <a href="https://www.wma.net/policies-post/declaration-on-euthanasia-and-physician-assisted-suicide/">World Medical Association declaration</a> of October 2019, adopted in Tbilisi, defines euthanasia as deliberately administering a lethal substance, or carrying out an intervention, by which a physician causes the death of a patient with decision-making capacity, at that patient’s voluntary request. Separately, it says that a physician who respects the right to decline treatment does not act unethically, even if death follows the refusal. Dialysis that was not performed therefore says nothing by itself about the nature of the decision. The procedure, the indication, the will, the intent, and the care that continues have to be stated. This essay does not settle a criminal-law classification.</p>
<p>Being a relative, having access to information, and being authorized to decide are three positions. If the patient can decide, the family’s consent does not replace his, and the family’s refusal does not cancel his. Section 6(7) belongs elsewhere. If a legal representative refuses consent, and that refusal conflicts with the interest of a person incapable of giving informed consent, the provider may apply to the court. Until the court decides, only procedures necessary to save that person’s life may be performed. The provision does not apply to the refusal of an adult who can decide.</p>
<p>The separate exception in section 6(9)(d) is tied to mental illness, or to symptoms of a mental disorder, and to danger to self or others, or to a threatened serious deterioration. The patient’s unpleasantness does not by itself establish that finding. For institutional care under this letter, section 9(5) requires the admission to be reported within 24 hours to the court in whose district the facility sits. The court decides whether the reasons were lawful. Until that decision, only procedures necessary to save life and health, or to protect those nearby, may be performed. Admission without consent does not replace consent to dialysis.</p>
<p>After a valid refusal of one procedure, the question that remains is what the patient accepts. Treatment of symptoms, nursing care, the presence of people close to him, another conversation. A temporary measure, if he accepts it, has to be described together with its limits. It should not be presented as an equivalent substitute for dialysis. Where the prognosis is uncertain and he consents, the volume allows a time-limited trial of treatment, with the goal and the time of review set in advance. It relies on the second edition of the Renal Physicians Association recommendations, from 2010. That does not make them a Slovak legal protocol. It must be possible to leave the trial. Neither refusal of dialysis nor hostile behaviour indicates palliative sedation. Sedation meant to make possible a procedure the patient is resisting needs its own authorization. A measure to protect staff does not replace consent to dialysis.</p>
<p>The same person can have a different will. We have no right to pick, as the true one, the version that agreed with us. If today’s decision is free and informed, its difference from yesterday’s yes does not invalidate it. If delirium has disrupted today’s deciding, an older concrete wish has a different weight. What decides is the judgment of current capacity, and whether the older wish applies to today’s situation. A physician is responsible for the quality of the procedure. He does not own the outcome, or another person’s will. When a particular procedure may no longer be carried out, or cannot be, other help remains. The limit of one intervention should not become the end of care.</p>
<p><em>This is a commentary on the fourth volume of Sunday Metaphysics, not a treatment decision for an individual patient, and not a substitute for Act No. 576/2004 Coll., Notification No. 40/2000 Coll., or a judgment on a particular ward. The model situations in the volume do not identify individual patients.</em></p>
